{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/164756-old-stories-and-those-that-got-away","id":164756,"title":"Old Stories and Those That Got Away","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-06-10T06:14:15-07:00","id":164756,"title":"Old Stories and Those That Got Away","content":"Old Story<br /> <br /> I ran a Mage game once, a long time ago (the 90s), for a group of Traditionalists with one Glasswalker. I don't remember all the details, but the main plot line was about the Technocracy (1st ed.) having withdrawn from the war in their city. Although things were generally pretty tense, they had been easing the pressure to a great degree. At first this seemed like victory, but soon they realized that the constructs in the area were not dying out, but were in fact expanding vast tunnel networks underground. They were creating huge supercomputer centers, able to compile databases of all sorts of information and use it to locate and eliminate Traditionalists.<br /> <br /> The strangest thing was, this level of computing power shouldn't have been possible! (This was the 90s and I was a teenager) Through investigation and other techniques, they discovered these tunnels all led to the same gigantic cavern, which housed a powerful node and thousands upon thousands of unconscious human life forms. They did not expect to find the heart of the supercomputer in that cavern, but they did. Twenty thousand infant incubators with wires routed through their brains. They were being used as computers, as the brain is the most powerful computer known to man, and were in a state of suspended death.<br /> <br /> This caused a huge amount of in-party fighting, because one member of the party believed that this &quot;computer&quot; could be used for &quot;good&quot;. He argued (successfully) that until they had a way to disconnect the babies and keep them alive, it was more humane to let them continue to live. And if they were going to be there anyway... it was best that he controlled the computer so it couldn't be used for evil.<br /> <br /> Needless to say, after a few weeks of this they hatched a plot to kill him. The resulting chaos was some of the best RP fun I have ever had. Thankfully we were all very close friends, and he did this kind of stuff A LOT, so it didn't piss anyone off. <br /> <br /> <br /> Never Got to Play<br /> <br /> Although I have STd them, I never got a chance to play in a Sorcerer's Crusade game. Finding groups for this is practically impossible nowadays, so I probably won't get a chance. I always loved the setting though... and the natural ability to scale it anywhere from High Fantasy to dirty truth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/PbrO9Kq.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2211-sixfootchicken","name":"SixFootChicken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2211_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"559"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}